One rationale for this assignment is as follows. Field_2 has the highest bird‐threat level (0.14) and needs 4 drones for full protection. Drones that are close to Field_2 – for example, Drone_1 (at 15,37, which lies inside Field_2) and Drone_3 – are assigned there. To complete the quartet required for full protection on Field_2, we also use Drone_4 and Drone_7 even though they are not as close as the first two, but they offer acceptable travel times.

Field_3 has the next highest risk (0.10) and requires 2 drones. Among the remaining drones, Drone_8 (at 35,14) and Drone_2 are the ones that produce lower overall travel costs to Field_3’s area.

That leaves the two remaining drones (Drone_5 and Drone_6) to be assigned to Field_1. Although Field_1 (threat level 0.07) requires 4 drones for full protection, assigning these two still offers partial protection, which is “better than no protection” even if not ideal. Field_4 (threat level 0.05) is the lowest‐priority field in this scenario, so no drone has been allocated there.

This grouping attempts to preserve the highest value protection – fully protecting the fields with the highest threat – while using the remaining drones to yield some benefit on the next most critical field.
