One reasonable approach is to leave the highest‐threat field (Field_2, threat level 0.30) with all 4 drones (two already protecting and two in transit) so that it is fully protected. Field_3 is already exactly covered (full protection with 2 drones) even though its threat level is a bit lower (0.05). That leaves Field_1 and Field_4 – both at a moderate threat level of 0.07 – for which we have only 2 drones available. Rather than splitting them (which would leave both only partly protected and nearly as vulnerable), we can concentrate the two available drones on Field_1 which already has 2 protecting drones. That way Field_1 would have 4 drones (full protection) and would deter the birds completely. Field_4, with its 0.07 threat level, would remain unprotected for now but—in a situation with only 8 total drones—it is often better to “save” the ones that can be made to fully cover one field (thus eliminating its damage) rather than partially cover two fields (where neither benefit fully from the scare‐effect). 

Any alternative assignment (for example, shifting one drone to Field_4 so that both Field_1 and Field_4 have one extra drone) would leave both fields only partly protected. Since partial protection is “not very effective,” concentrating your limited assets to achieve full protection on as many fields as possible (especially those where birds are already counted by the sensors) is a sensible strategy.

The assignment above therefore represents one good allocation.
