One way to explain this decision is as follows. The four fields have different bird‐threat levels (Field_2 = 0.19, Field_1 = 0.14, Field_3 = 0.10, and Field_4 = 0.03) and each field requires a fixed number of drones to be fully protected so that the birds will actually flee. With only 8 drones available we cannot cover all fields completely. If a field is not fully protected the birds (though a little scared by a partial show of force) will still land and cause crop damage. Therefore, it makes sense to “spend” our resources so that the fields with the highest threat levels are completely covered. In this case the two highest‐priority fields are Field_2 and Field_1 (with threat levels of 0.19 and 0.14, respectively) and they require 4 drones each. Since the four drones already at Field_1 (Drone_4–Drone_7) provide full protection there, we reassign the drones currently protecting the lower‐threat fields (Drone_1–Drone_3 from Field_4 and Drone_8 from Field_3) to Field_2. This leaves Field_3 (threat 0.10) and Field_4 (threat 0.03) unprotected – a combined risk of 0.13 – which is lower than leaving Field_2 (risk 0.19) unprotected.
