One good way to think about the decision was to note that with only eight drones available—and full protection defined only when the required number of drones is present—we cannot cover all four fields completely (the totals for full protection would require 4 + 4 + 2 + 3 = 13 drones). Given the sensor‐data threat levels (Field_4: 0.25; Field_2: 0.09; Field_3: 0.05; Field_1: 0.05), we want to concentrate the scarce assets (full protection) in the higher–threat fields.

Originally the drones were distributed as follows:
• Field_2 had 3 drones (but needs 4),
• Field_3 had 2 (just enough),
• Field_4 had 3 (just enough)
• Field_1 had 0.
If we left the distribution as is, Field_2 would remain vulnerable (threat 0.09 not deterred) while Field_3 and Field_4 would be secure and Field_1 would be unprotected (threat 0.05).

Because Field_2’s 0.09 threat level is higher than Field_3’s 0.05, we can “borrow” one drone from Field_3 and assign it to Field_2. That adjustment gives Field_2 its full complement (4 drones) so the birds over there will be driven off, while Field_3 (with threat 0.05) remains partially covered by 1 drone rather than having Field_2 remain underprotected. (No drone is assigned to Field_1 because none would be “close by” and the immediate threat there is lower.)

Thus the assignment chosen above minimizes overall risk given the current measurements.
